  Run one lever at a time, on the gold you already have, and spend the next\
  dollars only on the stage that just failed. The two full runs already paid for\
  the first discovery. A scored end-to-end baseline comes after a slice has a\
  before-and-after, not before.\
\
  The arc that demonstrates the work is: score what you have, fix the one worst\
  stage, rerun only that slice, then leave the next lever named. On GLM that arc\
  is about $1\'962. A full-corpus run is $0.90 and mixes every stage together, so it\
  is a poor place to learn.\
\
  1. Score the artifacts you already have. $0. Run the deterministic checks on\
  both run directories: schema, surfaced quotes, conservation, change-item\
  closure, scope completeness. You already know three failures without another\
  model call. Scope kept 63 and 44 stacks against gold lists of about a dozen and\
  four. Analysts spent 103 calls trying to re-read the trigger. commit_finding\
  failed on bad change_refs and citations outside the stack. The trigger-file fix\
  is already in the harness. Validate it by rerunning one stack that made those\
  calls, not the corpus.\
\
  2. Stage 4 on GLM, teacher-forced, k=1. About $0.20. Inject the gold change\
  record and run only the stacks that carry a distinct failure: Great Plains hop\
  and the Metals Adjustment decoy, Valley\'92s open clock, Cascade\'92s missed clock,\
  Bridgewell\'92s missing escalation, Sunpoint\'92s expired-but-surviving right, the\
  Keystone 2019 pin, the California technician template against Marchetti, Ellison\
  where abstaining is correct, and the utility-tariff decoy. This is the map. A\
  hop miss, a decoy hit, and a wrong clock are three different corrections.\
\
  3. Hill-climb one prompt, then rerun only the misses. The analyst prompt is\
  meant to be changed on its own. Change it for whichever slice failed hardest in\
  step 2, rerun those stacks, then rerun the whole step-2 set once to see what the\
  change broke. That is the correction-and-validation beat. Do not also swap the\
  model or the tool list in that run.\
\
  4. Stage 1 and stage 3, one call each, on the gold triggers. T1 on Proclamation\
  11021, the T3 pair, T5 with both California instruments, TR-03, and TR-90. These\
  calls are cents. Stage 1 asks whether the substantive item is found and the\
  distractor is dismissed. Stage 3 asks whether every gold stack is included and\
  whether the utility lease and the insurance policies are pruned. The full runs\
  showed weak pruning. Tighten the scope prompt only if this call shows a gold\
  stack kept in for a bad reason. Default-in stays until a real miss says\
  otherwise. Stage 0 is a unit test on the three pairs you already wrote, and it\
  costs nothing.\
\
  5. Labels, on the evidence from step 2, not a new reading pass. Score urgency,\
  materiality, and the dangerous swaps (material called not material, a live\
  forfeitable clock called anything else). If the spans were right and the labels\
  were wrong, the next experiment is the labeller. Jev belongs here: same\
  findings, classify instead of self-labels, on those items only. Sweep Jev\'92s\
  abstention threshold only if a dangerous swap remains. If the spans were wrong,\
  Jev will not help, and you stay on the step-3 prompt.\
\
  6. Stage 5 on injected gold findings, ranking approach A. One call for T1 and\
  one for the T3 pair. Check that tier 1 sits above tier 3, that tier 1 is not\
  filed as not material, and that the Sunpoint\'96Northbrook link is found. The\
  credit-agreement covenant must not show up on T3 or T5. Approach A is the\
  deterministic sort you already run. B (the model ranks freely) and C (reorder\
  only inside ties) are the one-time comparison, and only if A fails those checks.\
  The \'93full findings versus a summary plus fetch_stack\'94 handoff is the tool\
  change to try if the cross-link is the thing that fails.\
\
  7. The same slice on DeepSeek V4.1 Flash, k=1. Same stacks, same gold inputs.\
  This is the model lever. The two models cost about the same per full run, so the\
  comparison is the slice, not another corpus pass. A mixed config, one model for\
  the orchestrator and another for the stacks, is justified only if this shows\
  the two roles failing differently.\
\
  8. Repeat the winner. k=5 on the open-weight model that won the slice, sentinel\
  items only. That is how you show a fix stayed fixed. Luna, on the ChatGPT\
  subscription, is the closed reference after that: k=3, marginal cost about zero,\
  limited by rate limits. Sonnet is one k=1 pass over the same slice, and only if\
  Luna also scores low. At these prices a Sonnet pass over the whole corpus is\
  about $14, so it stays on the slice.\
\
  After that, the cheap metamorphics reuse the artifacts: the T3 date pair (labels\
  and tiers must move together), the Washington profile swap (the gate must\
  flip), and one clause deletion on a single stack (the finding must flip).\
  Scaling the corpus and renaming files are the later path. So is gold you have\
  not written yet: the Hai Phong hop, the Illinois band, and the Northaire pin.\
