  Yes. A single sample from a model cannot separate a stable miss from a one-off\
  draw, and the cheap stages should not be run at k=1 once you are trying to learn\
  anything.\
\
  k=1 is only a wiring check: the scorer runs, the gold loads, and a stack returns\
  a schema-valid finding. A pass there means the harness worked. It does not mean\
  the stage is reliable, and a fail there might not reproduce.\
\
  k=3 is the smallest number that gives a trend on these calls. The same item\
  failing the same way three times is a failure worth a prompt change. One fail\
  and two passes is variance, and you leave the prompt alone. Three passes means\
  it is not a consistent miss. You still do not have a rate, and you do not need\
  one to decide whether to hill-climb.\
\
  Stages 1, 3, and 5, and the label check, are one call each. Running each gold\
  trigger three times costs cents, so they should start at k=3. Stage 4 is the\
  expensive one, and the slice is still cheap there: about ten stacks at roughly\
  two cents each is about $0.20 per pass, so k=3 is about $0.60. That replaces the\
  k=1 step in the earlier order. The correction run uses the same k=3 on the\
  items that failed at least twice, then one k=3 pass over the whole slice to see\
  what the change broke.\
\
  k=5 stays where the design put it: on the open-weight model that won, after a\
  fix, to show the result held. Luna stays at k=3 because the subscription is\
  rate-limited. Nothing in this loop needs k=30.\
